In one of the deadliest attacks in the history of the Troubles, the Real IRA detonated a bomb in Omagh, Northern Ireland on August 15, 1998, leaving 29 people dead and over 200 injured. The bombing, which targeted the town's busy shopping district, sent shockwaves throughout the region and the world, leaving a trail of devastation and grief in its wake. Now, more than two decades later, some of the victims and their families are taking legal action against the alleged chiefs of the Real IRA, seeking justice and closure.

Background:

